{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 New York Yankees were a team that never quite found itself, lurching between flashes of brilliance and prolonged stretches of mediocrity that ultimately kept them out of the postseason for the first time in several years. Built around Aaron Judge and a roster of veteran pieces, the club looked the part on paper but too often failed to execute when it mattered most, finishing a disappointing 82-80 and watching October from their couches.\n\nThe season opened with equal measures of hope and heartbreak. A brutal Opening Day double play by Giancarlo Stanton set an ominous early tone, but the Yankees steadied themselves through April with clutch moments from young Anthony Volpe, who arrived in the Bronx as a legitimate star in the making, and veteran grit from Oswaldo Cabrera. May became the team's best month — a 19-10 run fueled by Aaron Judge home runs, an Anthony Rizzo walk-off blast, and Harrison Bader stepping up in big spots. For a few weeks in late spring, this looked like a playoff team.\n\nThe summer, however, told a darker story. July (10-15) and August (10-18) were catastrophic, and the culprit was painfully clear: Clay Holmes and the bullpen simply fell apart. Holmes allowed a grand slam, a walk-off single, and a damaging triple in a stretch that defined the team's slide. The All-Star break brought no reset. Instead, the losses piled up through a brutal August schedule, and by Labor Day the Yankees' playoff hopes had dwindled to a mathematical prayer.\n\nSeptember brought a remarkable but ultimately meaningless revival. The team went 17-10, with Giancarlo Stanton finally healthy and homering, Anthony Volpe reaching 20 home runs, and even Clay Holmes redeeming himself with a key double play to close out a win. The Yankees won games they needed to win — they just needed to have won them three months earlier. A 82-80 record and a missed playoff berth was the sobering final verdict on a season of wasted potential.",
  "chapters": [
